Yes, almost certainly the issue is "objects in one
list".

The problem isn't a NetHack problem, it is a C
compiler problem.

The code built by the compiler isn't robust enough,
say, to allow that many memory allocations to extend
a single list.

Probably the issue is down in the "buddy system" C
heap (mis)management, but at any length, the right
response is to design around the problem that _is_
the problem, failing to maintain long lists
properly, not to attempt to prevent the creation of
a long list anywhere in NetHack, an approach
almost certainly doomed from the outset.

The DevTeam has been patching around the various
crashes due to various long lists, that I've seen,
for as long as NetHack has existed, and still the
bugs reveal themselves again and again.

